    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Drawn to the Deep End is Gene’s second studio album, released in 1997 on Polydor and often regarded as the point where the band expanded beyond the sharp, Smiths-influenced Britpop of their debut into something more lavish and emotionally expansive. Built around Martin Rossiter’s dramatic vocals and the band’s strong melodic instincts, it pairs jangling guitars with fuller arrangements, string touches, and a more ambitious production style that gives the record a grand, melancholy sweep.\n\nLyrically, the album is steeped in loneliness, yearning, and emotional strain, which helped distinguish it from many of its Britpop-era peers. Singles such as “Fighting Fit,” “Where Are They Now?,” and “Speak to Me Someone” helped the album reach the UK Top 10, and it was well received for its depth and confidence, with critics noting that Gene had made a more opulent and fully realized record than their debut.",
